1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:29,327
MATTHEW: I went to school here, 1997 to 2001. I've walked Court Street hundreds of times. I bought all the beer, buggied all the burritos, and cheered the Bobcats. I walked Mulberry Street every week, and there was no sign. I called myself anti-racist then, and I still do. And I never once heard the name Christopher Davis. This film is about how a town forgets, and how even the people who think they're paying attention never get told.

2
00:00:38,843 --> 00:00:48,914
MATTHEW: That's my Bobcats, fall 1999. I was a junior. I knew the fight song, I knew Peden Stadium, and I knew every bar on Court Street. What I didn't know was a name.

3
00:00:57,214 --> 00:01:06,124
RAY ABERNATHY: Now, I grew up on Athens being the good guys. Underground Railroad town. Folks hid people in their cellars. That's what my granddad told me, anyway.

4
00:01:06,424 --> 00:01:23,639
MATTHEW: He's not wrong about the pride. Athens does remember itself as a haven. But outside Baker University Center stands a dark blue marker from the Equal Justice Initiative. EJI has documented at least fifteen racial terror lynchings in Ohio. One of them happened a few hundred feet from where students buy their textbooks.

5
00:01:23,939 --> 00:01:28,329
RAY ABERNATHY: Here? In Athens? I figured that sort of thing was a Southern story.

6
00:01:28,629 --> 00:01:43,347
MARCUS VANCE: Ray, I've got the 1880 census open. Lee Township, Athens County. Christopher C. Davis, twenty-four, farm laborer near Albany. Wife Kenziah. A three-year-old daughter, Robertie, and a baby boy, Silas. That's an Ohio family.

7
00:01:43,647 --> 00:02:02,094
MATTHEW: In late October 1881, a white neighbor, Lucinda Luckey, said Davis had raped her and struck her with an axe. She said she knew him by his lantern. Davis said he was innocent. A neighbor, Silas Julius, swore Davis was at his house that evening. And a few years earlier, when a fire wrecked her home, it was the Davis family who took her in.

8
00:02:02,394 --> 00:02:05,402
RAY ABERNATHY: Well, there was a trial, though. The law handled it.

9
00:02:05,702 --> 00:02:15,475
MARCUS VANCE: The sheriff knew better than that. He moved Davis fifty miles away to Chillicothe for his own safety. They brought him back on November twentieth for trial. He never got one.

10
00:02:15,775 --> 00:02:28,509
MATTHEW: Around two twenty in the morning on November twenty-first, 1881, a mob at least thirty strong, community leaders among them, broke into the Athens County jail. They dragged Christopher Davis to the South Bridge over the Hocking River, and they hanged him.

11
00:02:28,809 --> 00:02:40,202
MATTHEW: It did not end at the river. Davis was buried at West State Street Cemetery. Grave robbers dug him up without his family's consent and shipped his body to Starling Medical College in Columbus, to be cut apart for anatomy class.

12
00:02:40,502 --> 00:02:53,076
MARCUS VANCE: A grand jury sat. Not one indictment. Townspeople later named men in that mob. One was the accuser's own nephew. He went on to be the county's probate judge.

13
00:02:53,376 --> 00:02:55,586
RAY ABERNATHY: Nobody? Not one name?

14
00:02:55,886 --> 00:02:56,785
MARCUS VANCE: Not one.

15
00:02:57,085 --> 00:03:11,497
MATTHEW: For well over a century, that was the official record: silence. It took a community coalition and an OU graduate student, Jordan Zdinak, to dig it back up. Their research led to the soil ceremony at the bridge in 2019 and the marker on Mulberry Street in 2020.

16
00:03:11,797 --> 00:03:14,047
RAY ABERNATHY: My granddad never told me that one.

17
00:03:14,347 --> 00:03:25,256
MATTHEW: No. Most granddads didn't. Nobody told me either. When I was an undergrad, that marker didn't exist yet. Davis was killed a short walk from where I went to class, and I never knew.

18
00:03:29,556 --> 00:03:37,766
RAY ABERNATHY: Alright, but that was 1881. By the 1900s, the university was a different animal. College town. Open doors.

19
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:55,244
MATTHEW: The doors were open the way a ledger is open: on paper, and only on the right page. In 1923 the Ohio University trustees adopted a rule barring out-of-state students who couldn't attend their own home state's public universities. That described almost exactly one group: Black students from the Jim Crow South.

20
00:03:55,544 --> 00:04:03,033
MARCUS VANCE: The policy never says Black. It doesn't have to. Same with the College of Education, which quietly stopped placing Black student teachers in local classrooms.

21
00:04:03,333 --> 00:04:06,013
RAY ABERNATHY: Huh. So nobody had to say it out loud.

22
00:04:06,313 --> 00:04:11,214
MARCUS VANCE: That's the trick, Ray. Write it down plain enough and nobody ever has to say it out loud.

23
00:04:11,514 --> 00:04:21,077
MATTHEW: And the old way hadn't gone anywhere. That same spring, the Klan paper The Fiery Cross reported on a large Klan funeral in Glouster and recruiting drives across Athens County.

24
00:04:21,377 --> 00:04:24,131
RAY ABERNATHY: Glouster? My cousin's from Glouster.

25
00:04:24,431 --> 00:04:26,569
MARCUS VANCE: Then your cousin's town was in the paper.

26
00:04:26,869 --> 00:04:38,334
MATTHEW: By mid-century, local historians describe Athens as a very unwelcoming town to African Americans. Black students were refused service in Court Street restaurants and steered to off-campus housing on the west side.

27
00:04:38,634 --> 00:04:41,063
RAY ABERNATHY: Now, Court Street's where everybody goes.

28
00:04:41,363 --> 00:04:42,919
MARCUS VANCE: Everybody who got served.

29
00:04:43,219 --> 00:04:57,544
MARCUS VANCE: Members of all races are admitted to the University and to the use of its facilities on an equal basis. ... However, it is believed that Administrators should make every reasonable effort to achieve the greatest degree of happiness and satisfaction to the greatest number of people.

30
00:04:57,844 --> 00:05:01,144
RAY ABERNATHY: Well, that sounds fair enough. Equal basis.

31
00:05:01,444 --> 00:05:13,695
MARCUS VANCE: Read the next sentence, Ray. The greatest number of people meant the white students. Black students would board in private homes in town. And if enough of them came, the policy said, perhaps a cottage could be set aside for them.

32
00:05:13,995 --> 00:05:29,224
MATTHEW: President Walter Sylvester Gamertsfelder's administration approved that policy in 1944. Even after Black students moved into the same dorms, the housing form kept a race preference checklist. Housing wasn't officially desegregated until 1963.

33
00:05:29,524 --> 00:05:46,836
MATTHEW: Then there's the Berry. In 1892, Edward and Mattie Berry, Black entrepreneurs, opened a hotel on North Court Street. By every account it was the finest in town, and it welcomed everyone. Ohio University bought it in 1961. In 1974 the university knocked it down for a parking lot.

34
00:05:47,136 --> 00:05:49,054
RAY ABERNATHY: Well, a town needs parking.

35
00:05:49,354 --> 00:05:54,293
MARCUS VANCE: The lot was never built, Ray. It's Hangover Easy now. The out of place streetcar diner.

36
00:05:54,593 --> 00:05:56,548
RAY ABERNATHY: Then what'd they tear it down for?

37
00:05:56,848 --> 00:05:59,207
MATTHEW: That's the question this whole film keeps asking.

38
00:06:03,507 --> 00:06:11,135
RAY ABERNATHY: Okay. I'll grant you the old days. But that's history. Nobody's tearing anything down now. We put up a marker, didn't we?

39
00:06:11,435 --> 00:06:15,289
SIMONE BAPTISTE: We did, Ray. In 2020. Want to see what happened five years later?

40
00:06:15,589 --> 00:06:17,034
RAY ABERNATHY: I'm guessing I don't.

41
00:06:17,334 --> 00:06:28,664
MATTHEW: If history teaches anything, it's that institutions rarely need force to give in. They need a political shift and an instinct for self-preservation. In 2025 that played out across Ohio fast.

42
00:06:28,964 --> 00:06:41,032
SIMONE BAPTISTE: March twenty-eighth, 2025. Governor Mike DeWine signs Senate Bill 1, the Advance Ohio Higher Education Act. It bans DEI offices at public universities. It takes effect June twenty-seventh.

43
00:06:41,332 --> 00:06:43,468
RAY ABERNATHY: So the university had till summer.

44
00:06:43,768 --> 00:06:56,703
SIMONE BAPTISTE: It had till summer. It didn't use it. That spring, Ohio University shut its Division of Diversity and Inclusion. The Multicultural Center, the Pride Center and the Women's Center inside Baker University Center were shut down within weeks.

45
00:06:57,003 --> 00:07:07,688
MATTHEW: On April 29, OU President Lori Stewart Gonzalez wrote to the campus. The task ahead, she said, was, quote, not to look for ways to recreate the same approaches under a different name.

46
00:07:07,988 --> 00:07:18,526
RAY ABERNATHY: Under a different name. Huh. That's the 1923 trick backwards, isn't it? Back then you didn't say the word. Now you can't say the word.

47
00:07:18,826 --> 00:07:20,825
SIMONE BAPTISTE: Now you're doing my job, Ray.

48
00:07:21,125 --> 00:07:30,222
MATTHEW: OU was not alone, and it was never only about diversity offices. In 2025 a kind of weather settled over American institutions. Call it the aura of capitulation.

49
00:07:30,522 --> 00:07:36,996
SIMONE BAPTISTE: Ohio State closed two diversity offices in February 2025, a month before Senate Bill 1 was even signed.

50
00:07:37,296 --> 00:07:42,233
RAY ABERNATHY: Now hold on. Ohio State? My whole family bleeds scarlet and gray.

51
00:07:42,533 --> 00:07:46,373
SIMONE BAPTISTE: Then pour 'em a cup of coffee and sit 'em down, Ray. They're in the record too.

52
00:07:46,673 --> 00:07:55,868
RAY ABERNATHY: Well, schools live on state money. What do you expect? Now a big-city law firm, those fellas would fight. Fighting's what they bill for.

53
00:07:56,168 --> 00:08:18,713
SIMONE BAPTISTE: You'd think so. March 14, 2025. An executive order targets Paul, Weiss, one of the richest law firms in the country. It goes after the firm's security clearances, its access to federal buildings, its clients' contracts. Six days later the firm has a deal: $40 million in free legal work for causes the White House supports. The order is withdrawn.

54
00:08:19,013 --> 00:08:38,576
MATTHEW: The firm's chairman told his people the order could have destroyed the firm. Maybe so. But other firms that got the same kind of order went to court, and they won. Judges threw the orders out. And after Paul, Weiss, more big firms signed deals of their own, close to a billion dollars in promised work. Some of them signed before anyone had come for them.

55
00:08:38,876 --> 00:08:40,978
RAY ABERNATHY: Before anybody even asked.

56
00:08:41,278 --> 00:08:43,414
SIMONE BAPTISTE: Before anybody even asked.

57
00:08:43,714 --> 00:08:49,050
RAY ABERNATHY: Alright, but trimming government fat, I'm for that. My granddad never wasted a nail.

58
00:08:49,350 --> 00:09:10,224
SIMONE BAPTISTE: Your granddad measured before he cut, Ray. February 2025. The Department of Government Efficiency fires hundreds of people at the agency that looks after the nuclear weapons stockpile. Within days the government is trying to hire them back, and it can't reach some of them, because their email was already shut off. That same month, the Agriculture Department fires people working on bird flu, then says it's trying to get them back.

59
00:09:10,524 --> 00:09:22,300
MATTHEW: DOGE posted what it called a wall of receipts. Its biggest single item claimed $8 billion saved. The contract was worth $8 million. And the Congress that had voted for that spending mostly watched.

60
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,990
RAY ABERNATHY: Measure twice, cut once.

61
00:09:25,290 --> 00:09:28,450
SIMONE BAPTISTE: They cut first, Ray. Then they went looking for the tape measure.

62
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:34,341
RAY ABERNATHY: Okay, but a map? That's just a map. Who cares what a phone calls a body of water?

63
00:09:34,641 --> 00:09:49,615
SIMONE BAPTISTE: January twentieth, 2025. Executive Order 14172, Restoring Names That Honor American Greatness. It tells federal agencies to call the Gulf of Mexico the Gulf of America. That's federal agencies, Ray. Not companies.

64
00:09:49,915 --> 00:09:59,615
MATTHEW: Legal scholars were clear: the order bound the federal government, not private businesses. The name Gulf of Mexico had been in use for more than four centuries. Google didn't have to do a thing.

65
00:09:59,915 --> 00:10:11,662
SIMONE BAPTISTE: January twenty-eighth, Google says it'll follow the federal database. February tenth, Google Maps shows Gulf of America to everyone in the United States. People flood the listing with angry reviews, and Google turns reviews off.

66
00:10:11,962 --> 00:10:15,592
MATTHEW: I'll say it plainly, as my opinion: Google went full chicken.

67
00:10:15,892 --> 00:10:17,267
RAY ABERNATHY: Nobody made 'em.

68
00:10:17,567 --> 00:10:20,396
SIMONE BAPTISTE: Nobody made any of them, Ray. That's the story.

69
00:10:20,696 --> 00:10:26,577
RAY ABERNATHY: You know what gets me? I always figured the bad part of history was the loud part. The rope. The torches.

70
00:10:26,877 --> 00:10:37,239
MATTHEW: From the South Bridge in 1881 to the trustees' rule of 1923, the wrecking crews of 1974 and the boardrooms of 2025, the machinery hasn't changed much.

71
00:10:37,539 --> 00:10:46,228
MATTHEW: Exclusion and erasure don't always come with a rope or a torch. Now they come as a PDF memo, a locked door in a student center, a new label on a map.

72
00:10:46,528 --> 00:10:53,469
MATTHEW: When universities and tech giants bend the knee before anyone asks, erasure needs no mob. It needs only compliance.

73
00:10:53,769 --> 00:10:56,706
RAY ABERNATHY: I'm gonna go read that sign on Mulberry Street.

74
00:10:57,006 --> 00:10:59,617
MATTHEW: Me too, Ray. Twenty-five years late.
